{
  "abstract": "We bound quadratic jet determinants by retaining their complete finite polynomial row space. Disjoint-disk sampling compares the discrete row Gram matrix with a continuous Sobolev form. Classical disk-polynomial identities make all derivative forms diagonal in the same monic basis when order $j$ uses weight $(1-|z|^2/T^2)^{\\alpha+j}$. Spatial column prices account for available derivative prefixes, including unequal capacities. The finite theorem retains the sampling factor, area Jacobian, radius enlargement, weight variation and restored prices.\n\nFor triangular supports, exact grid-cell counts are quadratic polynomials in the degree quotient for every residue class. Their signed weighted sum divided by the square of the degree has a rational supremum determined by an endpoint, a limit and at most one critical point. This yields a fixed-size certificate uniform over every integer degree above an explicit threshold, including the varying integer parameter $\\alpha=\\lfloor S/2\\rfloor$.\n\nCombined with published Wronskian rank and local valuation results, the Jacobi family proves $D<18000$ under the class-number-one hypothesis. An elementary factorial bound, finite prime sum and forty norm integrals give the complete arithmetic comparison without asymptotic prime estimates. We also extract the stronger consequence $D<17991$ from an earlier published Wronskian–Meixner inequality, and a separate construction already gives $D<12100$. At fixed $D=587$, a prescribed Jacobi parameter lowers the complete volume upper from $0.255348356498032$ to $0.246023649166378$ at unchanged arithmetic and geometric resources, producing gap greater than $0.0156026258200455$.\n\nExact rational witnesses, directed CPU certificates and fresh arithmetic replays accompany the proofs. Classical sampling and orthogonal-polynomial ingredients remain credited. No new overall discriminant endpoint, lower finite bridge, census, classification or optimality is claimed.",
